screen.CLOUD_REALISTIC_SETTINGS=§9§lNubis Extended (beta)
screen.CLOUD_REALISTIC_SETTINGS.comment=The volumetric cloud style. §e[*]§r Only does anything with Cloud Style set to §bRealistic§r. §c[-]§r This is a ray march through a real slab of atmosphere and costs a great deal more than the other styles.
option.VC_GENUS=Cloud Genus
option.VC_GENUS.comment=Which kind of cloud this layer draws. §e[*]§r One genus at a time - the layer is a single generator and the genus is the set of numbers fed to it, so the sky shows one and only one. §e[*]§r §aCumulus§r is the default and is exactly the clouds the pack has always drawn. §aStratocumulus§r is the halfway house - the sheet has broken into rolls and patches that still touch each other. §aStratus§r goes the whole way: eroded four times less, no dome carved at all, a flat grey lid over the whole sky. §e[*]§r §aAltocumulus§r is the first that leaves the low deck - 994 blocks up instead of 284, half as deep, a third as opaque, and cut into separate elements by an erosion five times heavier than anything the low deck uses. It ships in patches - two or three regions of sky with clear air between them, drifting with the pressure field; §aAltocumulus Sky Regions§r in Weather is what widens them, and at 100 it covers the whole sky again. §e[*]§r §aCirrocumulus§r is the mackerel sky: a flat ripple of tiny white grains 2272 blocks up, in patches with clear sky between them, and so thin that it barely dims the sun. It is the only genus here with no 3D noise in it at all, and the cheapest to draw. §e[*]§r §aCirrus§r is the highest of them all at 2556 blocks: thin white fibres drawn out into streaks and hooks, so sparse that the sky stays blue through them. §e[*]§r §aCirrostratus§r is the same generator with its coverage at full and its erosion off: one continuous milky veil over the whole sky, thin enough that the sun keeps a visible disc through it. §e[*]§r §aCumulonimbus§r is the thunderhead: a single storm 2272 blocks tall standing in clear sky, with an anvil spreading out over its own base. Unlike every other genus its position is placed rather than grown, so there is normally exactly one in view. §e[*]§r §aAll§r is the sky itself rather than one kind of cloud in it. Low deck: cumulus, stratocumulus and stratus at the same time, in territories, blending from one into the next across the world. High above it, its own territories of cirrocumulus, cirrus and cirrostratus - so the sky overhead is not one kind of high cloud either. The two decks are independent of each other and move at different speeds, because the wind aloft is faster. §aSky Region Size§r and §aSky Character§r in Weather shape both. §e[*]§r This is where the second layer went: it was a debug switch, and a layer is not something to choose. §cAll is about twice the cost of a single genus§r - two marches through the sky, each with its own lighting. §e[*]§r Altocumulus and cumulonimbus are not in it yet: the first wants a third band of its own, and the second is a placed object rather than a field. §e[*]§r More genera are added to this list as they are built.
value.VC_GENUS.0=Cumulus
value.VC_GENUS.1=层云
value.VC_GENUS.2=Stratocumulus
value.VC_GENUS.3=Altocumulus
value.VC_GENUS.4=Cirrocumulus
value.VC_GENUS.5=Cirrus
value.VC_GENUS.6=Cirrostratus
value.VC_GENUS.7=Cumulonimbus
value.VC_GENUS.8=全部
option.VC_EXT_MIN=Extinction Minimum
option.VC_EXT_MIN.comment=The thinnest a cloud can be, as a percentage of §aExtinction§r above. §e[*]§r Extinction is how much light one step through cloud takes away, and it used to be one number for the whole sky - every cloud in it exactly as dense as every other. This is the bottom of the range each cloud draws its own from. §e[*]§r Set this and §aExtinction Maximum§r both to 100 for the old single number; the field is then not evaluated at all and it costs nothing.
option.VC_EXT_MAX=Extinction Maximum
option.VC_EXT_MAX.comment=The densest a cloud can be, as a percentage of §aExtinction§r above. §e[*]§r Wide ranges read as weather - thin hazy cloud next to something solid and dark-bottomed - and narrow ones as one air mass. §e[*]§r It reaches the lighting too, not just the look: a denser cloud is darker underneath and brighter where the sun catches it, because the light cone marches through the same density.
option.VC_H_EXTINCTION=High Cloud Extinction
option.VC_H_EXTINCTION.comment=Under §aCloud Genus: All§r, how dense the HIGH deck is - cirrus, cirrostratus, cirrocumulus - as a percentage of what those genera have on their own. §e[*]§r At 100 cirrus is as dense as the cloud under it, shades itself grey underneath, and the two decks run together into one mass. Real cirrus is optically thin, which is why it is white: light goes straight through it. §e[*]§r Lower is thinner and more see-through - the blue of the sky shows through it. It does NOT make the deck whiter; that is §aHigh Cloud Whiteness§r. §e[*]§r Only the high deck; the low one keeps §aExtinction§r as it is.
screen.SUNSET_SETTINGS=Sunset & Atmosphere
option.SUNSET_MODE=Sunset Colours
option.SUNSET_MODE.comment=The colour of the sky, the sunlight and the shadows at sunrise and sunset. The clouds follow, since they are lit by the same light. §e[*]§r Where a mode has an orange end, the orange is at the horizon by the sun and the other colour higher up - the light nearest the sun has crossed the most air, so it is always the warmest. §e[*]§r Only the colour changes between modes, not the brightness: each takes its brightness from the classic sunset for the biome you are in, so the exposure stays put. Rain greys every mode the same way. §e[*]§r §6R§ca§dn§5d§6o§cm§r picks one of the five each day. The day turns at midnight rather than at dawn, so a sunrise never changes colour halfway up, and that day's sunset matches it. §e[*]§r §7Classic§r is the sunset the pack had before: it was built by tinting the blue sky, which can only make it grey-lavender, never properly orange or red.
value.SUNSET_MODE.0=§aClassic
value.SUNSET_MODE.1=§6Orange§r
value.SUNSET_MODE.2=§6Ora§cnge t§do Vio§5let§r
value.SUNSET_MODE.3=§cScarlet§r
value.SUNSET_MODE.4=§cScarlet to Or§6ange§r
value.SUNSET_MODE.5=§cScar§dlet to Vi§5olet§r
value.SUNSET_MODE.6=§6R§ca§dn§5d§6o§cm§r
option.SUNSET_STRENGTH=Sunset Strength
option.SUNSET_STRENGTH.comment=How vivid the sunset colours are. §e[*]§r At §a100§r each part of the sky is allowed only a little more colour than the classic sunset had in the same place: the glow by the sun and the sky above it take most of it, the sunlight a little, and the shadows almost none, because they are lit by the blue sky. That is what a real sunset does - the zenith stays blue. §e[*]§r §a0§r keeps the classic amount of colour with the mode's hue; above 100 is stylised on purpose.
option.ATM_RAYLEIGH=Rayleigh Scattering
option.ATM_RAYLEIGH.comment=Scattering by the air itself - what makes the sky blue and a low sun red. §e[*]§r Drives the §aclouds§r: how much the sunlight reaching them reddens toward sunset and with depth, and the haze laid over distant cloud. The sky dome itself is painted by §aSunset Colours§r, not by this. §e[*]§r Higher reddens evening clouds sooner and harder.
option.ATM_RAYLEIGH_H=Rayleigh Height
option.ATM_RAYLEIGH_H.comment=How fast the air thins with altitude, in hundreds of metres: 80 is Earth's 8 km. Higher keeps more air above high cloud, so it reddens more like the low deck does.
option.ATM_MIE=薄雾
option.ATM_MIE.comment=Dust, droplets and smoke. Grey rather than blue, and held low down. Higher gives dimmer, more washed-out sunlight on the clouds near sunset and more haze over distant cloud.
option.ATM_MIE_H=Haze Height
option.ATM_MIE_H.comment=How high the haze reaches, in hundreds of metres: 12 is Earth's 1.2 km.
option.ATM_OZONE=臭氧层
option.ATM_OZONE.comment=Absorbs the middle of the spectrum. It is what keeps twilight light bluish-violet instead of letting it go grey; at 0 evening clouds turn more yellow, higher leans them toward violet.
option.ATM_THICKNESS=Atmosphere Thickness
option.ATM_THICKNESS.comment=How deep the whole atmosphere is, in km. Earth's is 100.
option.VC_H_SHADOW=High Cloud Shadows
option.VC_H_SHADOW.comment=Under §aCloud Genus: All§r, whether the HIGH deck shades the clouds below it. A sheet of cirrostratus dims the whole deck under it; a cirrus streak lays a soft grey band across the cumulus, offset from the streak itself toward the side away from the sun. §e[*]§r Cheap: the high deck is thin enough that its shadow is worked out in one step rather than traced. §e[*]§r §eDebug View 13§r shows it on its own.
value.VC_H_SHADOW.0=关
value.VC_H_SHADOW.1=开
option.VC_GROUND_SHADOW=Ground Shadow Strength
option.VC_GROUND_SHADOW.comment=How dark the volumetric clouds' shadows on the ground are. §e[*]§r Needs §aCloud Shadows§r on. §e[*]§r §a100§r is what the clouds really let through - a thick cumulus leaves a deep shadow, a thin one a pale one, and the shadows travel with the wind. §e[*]§r They come from the same clouds you see, so a shadow's edge lines up with the cloud's edge when you look toward the sun.
option.VC_REFLECT=云层倒影
option.VC_REFLECT.comment=The Realistic clouds reflected in water, in every water style. §e[*]§r Marched again for every water pixel - turn it off or lower the quality if a lake costs too much.
value.VC_REFLECT.0=关
value.VC_REFLECT.1=开
option.VC_REFLECT_QUALITY=Cloud Reflection Quality
option.VC_REFLECT_QUALITY.comment=How carefully the reflected clouds are marched. §e[*]§r §aLow§r: three times longer steps, one light sample. §eMedium§r: twice as long, two samples. §cHigh§r: the full march - expensive.
value.VC_REFLECT_QUALITY.1=§a低配
value.VC_REFLECT_QUALITY.2=§a中配
value.VC_REFLECT_QUALITY.3=高
option.VC_NEAR_REFINE=Fine Steps Inside Clouds
option.VC_NEAR_REFINE.comment=Shorter march steps near your eyes, so a cloud you are inside looks smoother. §e[*]§r Expensive: roughly doubles the work while you are inside a cloud. Does nothing outside clouds.
value.VC_NEAR_REFINE.0=关
value.VC_NEAR_REFINE.1=开
option.VC_OBJ_SHADOW=Shadows in Clouds
option.VC_OBJ_SHADOW.comment=Players, mobs and blocks inside a cloud cast a shadow into it, away from the sun and away from beacon beams, the way the light travels. §e[*]§r Within about 30 blocks of you: further out a small object's shadow is filled in by the cloud's scattered light. §e[*]§r The sun's uses the shadow map and reaches as far as your shadows do; a beacon's uses the voxel volume round you - sharp within the fine voxel grid (Fine Voxel Color), blocky beyond it.
value.VC_OBJ_SHADOW.0=关
value.VC_OBJ_SHADOW.1=开
option.VC_OBJ_SHADOW_STRENGTH=云层阴影强度
option.VC_OBJ_SHADOW_STRENGTH.comment=How dark those shadows are, from the sun and from beacon beams. Light scattered by the rest of the cloud fills some of a shadow in, so it is never fully black.
option.VC_BEAM_LIGHT=Beacon Light
option.VC_BEAM_LIGHT.comment=Beacon beams light the clouds they pass through - inside the cloud and around it. §e[*]§r Up to eight beacons at once, including ones behind you.
value.VC_BEAM_LIGHT.0=关
value.VC_BEAM_LIGHT.1=开
option.VC_BEAM_BRIGHTNESS=Beacon Light Brightness
option.VC_BEAM_BRIGHTNESS.comment=How bright a beacon beam makes the clouds, percent.
option.VC_BEAM_RADIUS=Beacon Light Radius
option.VC_BEAM_RADIUS.comment=How far the glow spreads through the cloud around the beam, in blocks. §e[*]§r The bright core right around the beam is a fifth of this.
option.VC_BEAM_TINT=Beacon Glass Tint
option.VC_BEAM_TINT.comment=How much stained glass on the beacon colours the light in the clouds. §e[*]§r §a0§r is always white; §a100§r is the beam's own colour.
option.VC_SCENE_AMBIENT=Overcast Sky Light
option.VC_SCENE_AMBIENT.comment=How much a cloudy sky changes the light in the world. Under a clear sky the shade is lit blue; under overcast it is lit by the grey underside of the clouds, and a little less. §e[*]§r The amount of sky covered is measured around you and eased over a few seconds, so a passing cloud changes the light without a jump. §e[*]§r Needs §aCloud Shadows§r on. §a0§r turns it off.
option.VC_H_SHADOW_STRENGTH=High Cloud Shadow Strength
option.VC_H_SHADOW_STRENGTH.comment=How dark the high deck's shadow on the clouds below is. §e[*]§r §a100§r is physical, and on a sunlit deck it is barely visible - real cirrus lets most of the sun through, and what it takes is mostly made up by the sky's own light. §e[*]§r The default §a200§r is stylised so the two layers read apart; higher is stronger still.
option.VC_H_WHITENESS=High Cloud Whiteness
option.VC_H_WHITENESS.comment=Under §aCloud Genus: All§r, how white the HIGH deck is, separately from how see-through it is. §e[*]§r It is how little the deck shades itself: at §a0§r it is lit exactly like the cloud below it - grey inside, lavender underneath - and the two decks run together. Raising it lets sunlight and skylight reach its interior and underside, which is what makes real cirrus white. §e[*]§r It never makes the deck brighter than a sunlit thin cloud would be, so it cannot blow out the sky around the sun. §e[*]§r Only the high deck.
option.VC_EXT_VAR=Cloud-to-Cloud Variation
option.VC_EXT_VAR.comment=Whether every cloud in the sky is exactly as dense as every other. §e[*]§r With it ON each cloud draws its own extinction from the range below, so a thin one and a heavy one can stand side by side and the light that reaches you through each is different. §e[*]§r With it OFF one number covers the whole sky - the behaviour before this was built - and the field is not evaluated at all, so it costs nothing. §e[*]§r The three settings below do nothing while this is off.
value.VC_EXT_VAR.0=§cOFF
value.VC_EXT_VAR.1=§aON
option.VC_EXT_VAR_FREQ=Extinction Patch Size
option.VC_EXT_VAR_FREQ.comment=How large a patch of sky shares one extinction. §e[*]§r A frequency over the cloud domain, so a smaller number is a LARGER patch: §a8§r is about 1500 blocks at the default domain. §e[*]§r It should stay COARSER than a cloud. Finer than that and one cloud is part thin and part dense, which reads as a seam through it rather than as two clouds.
option.VC_REGION_FREQ=Sky Region Size
option.VC_REGION_FREQ.comment=Under §aCloud Genus: All§r, how large a territory of one kind of cloud is - in both decks, which have their own independent territories at the same size. §e[*]§r It is a frequency over the cloud domain, so a smaller number is a LARGER region - and it has to be a whole number, because the field tiles by wrapping its lattice and a fraction breaks the wrap. §e[*]§r Measured median width of one territory: §a2§r 6800 blocks, §a3§r 2800, §a4§r 2450, §a8§r 1150. The clouds are drawn out to 6000 blocks by default, so 4 puts two or three kinds of sky in one view; 1 or 2 is one kind of weather as far as you can see, changing over a long flight. §e[*]§r Has no effect unless Cloud Genus is All.
option.VC_RAIN_OVERCAST=Rain Brings Overcast
option.VC_RAIN_OVERCAST.comment=When it rains, the sky around you closes: over §aRain Cloud Gathering§r ticks the clouds out to about 4 km thicken into a solid, dark deck, whatever the day's cloudiness, and when the rain stops they open up again the same way. §e[*]§r Under §aCloud Genus: All§r the clouds there turn into a flat, stratus-like deck and the high clouds into a veil. §e[*]§r There is no true rain cloud (nimbostratus) yet - a thick, dark stratus stands in for it. §e[*]§r Follows §aPersonal Weather§r if that is set.
value.VC_RAIN_OVERCAST.0=关
value.VC_RAIN_OVERCAST.1=开
option.VC_RAIN_TICKS_IN=Rain Cloud Gathering
option.VC_RAIN_TICKS_IN.comment=How many game ticks the sky takes to close over when rain or a thunderstorm starts. §e[*]§r Game ticks, not seconds: §a1200§r is a minute at the normal tick rate, and a faster tick rate gathers the clouds faster.
option.VC_RAIN_TICKS_OUT=Rain Cloud Clearing
option.VC_RAIN_TICKS_OUT.comment=How many game ticks the rain clouds take to clear after the rain or storm ends. §e[*]§r Game ticks: §a2400§r is two minutes at the normal tick rate.
option.VC_RAIN_COVERAGE=Rain Coverage
option.VC_RAIN_COVERAGE.comment=How much of the sky is cloud under rain or a thunderstorm, as a percentage of the sky. §e[*]§r It applies whatever the day's cloudiness is - a clear day still closes over when it rains, and a cloudy one does not get any more closed than this says. §e[*]§r Measured, not nominal: §a80§r covers about four fifths of the sky and leaves thin places you can see daylight through, §a100§r seals it. Below §a40§r the rain falls out of a broken sky, which is not what rain looks like, but it is yours to choose. §c[-]§r The reading is for the shipped weather settings; §bIslands§r, §bCloud Pattern Size§r and §bAnisotropy§r reshape the field the percentage was measured on.
option.VC_REGION_COVER=Cloudiness Variation
option.VC_REGION_COVER.comment=Under §aCloud Genus: All§r, how much the AMOUNT of cloud changes from one part of the world to another. §e[*]§r At §a0§r the sky holds about the same amount of cloud everywhere - measured, 78 per cent of a view at the shipped coverage, whether the view is the emptiest place in the world or the fullest. Raising it lets the places the weather already calls clear actually be clear: at §a15§r a view ranges from a quarter covered to three quarters. §e[*]§r It works on the high deck too, which shares the low deck's pressure pattern - a low brings cloud to both. It lowers the average amount of cloud as well as spreading it, so it is not free - past about 20 the world is fair weather with occasional cloud rather than varied. §e[*]§r Most useful at high §aCoverage§r; at low coverage the sky is already thin everywhere and this only thins it further. §e[*]§r Costs nothing to draw - it is applied when the weather map is built, not while the clouds are marched.
option.VC_REGION_BIAS=Sky Character
option.VC_REGION_BIAS.comment=Under §aCloud Genus: All§r, which way the whole sky leans. §e[*]§r At §a0§r the three kinds get 30 / 40 / 30 per cent of the sky - the field's own distribution, straightened so that the middle kind does not swallow it. Negative is a fair-weather world of cumulus with the occasional overcast patch; positive is a grey one with the occasional break. §e[*]§r It leans BOTH decks the same way, which is the one thing the high territories and the low ones have in common: negative is cumulus under cirrocumulus and cirrus, positive is stratus under a cirrostratus veil. It moves which kind of cloud is where, not how much cloud there is - that is §aCoverage§r. §e[*]§r Has no effect unless Cloud Genus is All.
option.VC_LOOK=视觉效果
option.VC_LOOK.comment=Which set of shape and lighting numbers the march uses. §e[*]§r Every value the march reads is chosen here before it starts, so a look is a set of numbers rather than a separate renderer.
value.VC_LOOK.0=默认
value.VC_LOOK.1=浓密
value.VC_LOOK.2=Wispy
option.VC_DEBUG=调试视图
option.VC_DEBUG.comment=Replaces the clouds with one stage of their own calculation, so a bad-looking sky can be traced to the step that produced it instead of guessed at. §e[*]§r §e11§r and §e12§r are not stages -- they show the alpha and the colour the renderer hands back, which is where a layer that marches correctly but cannot be seen gives itself away. §e[*]§r Views that belong to a stage which is not built yet show flat magenta wherever the ray crosses the cloud layer. §e[*]§r Atmosphere tints, moon phase, aurora and nebula are all skipped while this is on, so what you see is the raw number.
value.VC_DEBUG.0=关
value.VC_DEBUG.1=1 Noise Slice
value.VC_DEBUG.2=2 Density
value.VC_DEBUG.3=3 Weather Map
value.VC_DEBUG.4=4 Cloud Top Height
value.VC_DEBUG.5=5 March Steps
value.VC_DEBUG.6=6 Sun Transmittance
value.VC_DEBUG.7=7 View Transmittance
value.VC_DEBUG.8=8 Single Scattering
value.VC_DEBUG.9=9 Multiple Scattering
value.VC_DEBUG.10=10 Temporal History
value.VC_DEBUG.11=§e11 Final Alpha
value.VC_DEBUG.12=§e12 Final Colour
value.VC_DEBUG.13=13 High Deck Shadow
value.VC_DEBUG.14=14 Cloud Physics
screen.CLOUD_REALISTIC_WEATHER=Sky & Weather Map
screen.CLOUD_REALISTIC_WEATHER.comment=The field that decides where clouds are allowed to exist at all, and what kind. §e[*]§r It is a 2D map over the sky, not a property of any one cloud. §e[*]§r View it with Debug View 3.
option.VC_DOMAIN=地图尺寸
option.VC_DOMAIN.comment=How many blocks across the whole weather map is before it repeats. §e[*]§r Smaller means more, smaller formations in view at once; larger means you see less of the map and the sky reads as one weather system. §e[*]§r It is also what sets how WIDE one cloud is against its own height. At the default a cloud is about twice as wide as it is tall, which is what a cumulus does; a third of this and the same cloud is a pillar. §e[*]§r The map tiles seamlessly at any size. §c[-]§r Below Cloud Distance the tile repeats inside the frame - the same formation shows up twice along one ray.
option.VC_MESO_FREQ=Formation Scale
option.VC_MESO_FREQ.comment=How many cloud formations fit across the map. §c[-]§r Raising it past about 12 pushes detail below what the map can hold and the field starts to look grainy rather than detailed. That ceiling is set by the map resolution against Map Size, so it moves if either does.
option.VC_WARP=Domain Warp
option.VC_WARP.comment=How much the map distorts its own lookup, which is what turns round blobs into the curled, folded fronts real weather has. §c[-]§r Too much is not more interesting, it is noise: the warped frequency runs past the map resolution and the field goes to salt and pepper. 35 gives the folding without that.
option.VC_TYPE_FREQ=Cloud Type Scale
option.VC_TYPE_FREQ.comment=Over what distance the sky changes its mind about what kind of cloud to make. §e[*]§r Cloud type runs from stratus, a thin flat sheet, through stratocumulus to cumulus, which fills the whole layer. At 1 or 2 a whole scene is one kind of day; higher values mix flat and towering cloud within a single view.

option.VC_COVERAGE_BIAS=Cloudiness
option.VC_COVERAGE_BIAS.comment=How far the air-mass gate is opened. §e[*]§r Low values give separate formations with clear sky between them; high values close the gaps until the sky is one unbroken deck. At the default the weather map is about one part in six covered, and no single formation holds more than a quarter of that.
option.VC_ANISO=Wind Stretch
option.VC_ANISO.comment=How far formations are stretched along the wind, as a whole-number ratio. §e[*]§r 1 is round. Higher values give the parallel bands real cumulus form into, which are called cloud streets. §e[*]§r It has to be a whole number: the stretch is what makes the map tile seamlessly, and only an integer transform does that. §c[-]§r Past 3 or 4 the map starts repeating inside itself, since the pattern covers its own tile once per unit of stretch.
option.VC_H_WIND_TURN=High Wind Turn
option.VC_H_WIND_TURN.comment=Under §aCloud Genus: All§r, how far the wind up at the HIGH deck is turned from the wind at the low one, in degrees, clockwise seen from above. §e[*]§r Real winds turn with height - where warm air is moving in they turn clockwise - so cirrus drifts across the cumulus rather than along it, and its streaks point the way it is going. §e[*]§r At §a0§r both decks travel the same way and the high one is only faster. It is always 2.5 times faster than the low deck.
value.VC_H_WIND_TURN.0=0§7°
value.VC_H_WIND_TURN.15=15§7°
value.VC_H_WIND_TURN.30=30§7°
value.VC_H_WIND_TURN.45=45§7°
value.VC_H_WIND_TURN.60=60§7°
value.VC_H_WIND_TURN.90=90§7°
value.VC_H_WIND_TURN.120=120§7°
value.VC_H_WIND_TURN.150=150§7°
value.VC_H_WIND_TURN.180=180§7°
option.VC_WIND_DIR=风向
option.VC_WIND_DIR.comment=Direction the weather travels, in degrees. Sets both the drift and the axis formations stretch along. §e[*]§r Eight directions rather than a free angle, and that is not a shortcut: the map only tiles seamlessly if the stretch is a whole-number transform, and only the four axes and four diagonals stay whole without the pattern repeating inside itself. §e[*]§r Diagonals come out slightly finer than the axes -- the stretch ratio is exact either way, only the overall scale shifts.
option.VC_WIND_SPEED=风速
option.VC_WIND_SPEED.comment=How fast the sky moves, in tenths of a block per second. §e[*]§r The default is about 12 metres per second at cloud level, which is a brisk but ordinary wind. Much above that and the sky reads as time-lapse rather than weather. §e[*]§r At realistic speeds the weather map in Debug View 3 looks almost still over half a minute. That is correct, not broken -- it spans several kilometres, and real weather takes minutes to cross it.
option.VC_EVOLVE=Evolution Speed
option.VC_EVOLVE.comment=How fast formations change shape, as opposed to how fast they travel. §e[*]§r This is deliberately far slower than the wind. Clouds that change as fast as they move look like a boiling sky, which is one of the most recognisable ways a procedural sky gives itself away. At the default the map turns over in roughly ten minutes.
option.VC_SEED=Weather Seed
option.VC_SEED.comment=Which weather pattern the pressure systems form. §e[*]§r At 0 it follows the in-game day, and CHANGES THROUGH it: today's pressure pattern, territories and cloud densities blend steadily into tomorrow's over the whole day, so there is never a moment the sky jumps. Go back to a day and the time of day, and it is the same sky. Thunderstorms keep their places and drift with the wind rather than moving at midnight. §e[*]§r Any other value pins one particular sky permanently, which is what you want for screenshots or a stream.
option.VC_SYNOPTIC=Pressure Influence
option.VC_SYNOPTIC.comment=How strongly the barometric field decides where clouds are allowed. §e[*]§r Low pressure means air converging and rising, which makes cloud; high pressure means it sinking, which clears the sky. At 0 the pressure systems stop mattering and cloudiness is uniform across the map.
option.VC_SYN_FLOW=Flow Bending
option.VC_SYN_FLOW.comment=How much the wind around the pressure centres bends the cloud field. §e[*]§r This is what curves a front around a low instead of leaving it a straight band. §e[*]§r It is a fixed distortion travelling with the centres, not something that accumulates -- a growing one would fold the map over itself.
screen.CLOUD_REALISTIC_MS=多重散射
screen.CLOUD_REALISTIC_MS.comment=Light that has bounced inside the cloud more than once, which is most of what makes a cloud look like a cloud rather than cotton wool. §e[*]§r Compare with Debug View 8 (single scattering) against Debug View 9 (what actually ships).
option.VC_MS_MODE=方法
option.VC_MS_MODE.comment=Which approximation to use. §e[*]§r §bOctaves§r sums several scattering orders, each reaching deeper and scattering more evenly than the last. §e[*]§r §bOctaves + Inner Glow§r trades the weakest octave for a term that lets deep cloud light up when you look through it at the sun. §c[-]§r They approximate the same energy, so do not think of them as stacking.
value.VC_MS_MODE.0=关
value.VC_MS_MODE.1=Octaves
value.VC_MS_MODE.2=Octaves + Inner Glow
option.VC_MS_OCTAVES=Scattering Orders
option.VC_MS_OCTAVES.comment=How many bounces are approximated. §e[*]§r Each one costs an exponential per lit sample and contributes half as much as the one before, so the third is already faint and the fifth is nearly free of consequence.
option.VC_MS_ATTEN=Depth Reach
option.VC_MS_ATTEN.comment=How much less each successive order is absorbed, so how much deeper into a cloud it reaches. §e[*]§r This is the setting that decides whether thick cloud has a lit interior or a black one. §e[*]§r 50 is also the fastest value by a noticeable margin: at exactly one half, every order can be derived from the first with a square root instead of its own exponential.
option.VC_MS_CONTRIB=Order Falloff
option.VC_MS_CONTRIB.comment=How much each successive order contributes. §e[*]§r The total is normalised, so this changes the balance between orders and never the overall brightness.
option.VC_MS_ECCENT=Directional Falloff
option.VC_MS_ECCENT.comment=How much more evenly each successive order scatters. §e[*]§r Low values make the higher orders almost directionless, which is what softens the silver lining into a glow rather than a rim.
option.VC_GLOW_DISTANCE=Glow Distance
option.VC_GLOW_DISTANCE.comment=Distance at which the inner glow reaches full strength, in blocks. §e[*]§r Only used by §bOctaves + Inner Glow§r. §e[*]§r Nearby cloud is deliberately left out of it.
screen.CLOUD_REALISTIC_LIGHT=Cloud Lighting
screen.CLOUD_REALISTIC_LIGHT.comment=How light gets through the cloud and to your eye. §e[*]§r Single scattering only for now: one bounce toward the sun, plus a flat ambient term. §e[*]§r Debug View 6 shows the sun shadow on its own, Debug View 8 shows the lit term.
option.VC_VIEW_OPACITY=View Opacity
option.VC_VIEW_OPACITY.comment=How solid the low cloud deck looks, as a multiple of its Extinction, applied to the line of sight only. §e[*]§r Raising it makes thick clouds read as solid white from above instead of letting rivers and fields show through, and keeps them solid at every Cloud Quality. The thin edges stay soft. §e[*]§r It does not change how the clouds are lit or their shadows on the ground, and the high clouds (cirrus) keep their natural translucency.
suffix.VC_VIEW_OPACITY=%
option.VC_EXTINCTION=Extinction
option.VC_EXTINCTION.comment=How strongly cloud material absorbs light, per block. §e[*]§r Raising it makes clouds read as denser and more opaque without changing their shape. §e[*]§r It applies to the view ray and the sun ray alike, so it deepens shadows as well.
option.VC_WORLD_SCALE=World Scale
option.VC_WORLD_SCALE.comment=How many metres of atmosphere one block is worth. §e[*]§r Minecraft is not built to scale, so at 1 the cloud deck sits 192 m up and sees almost exactly the same sunlight as the ground - no sunset separation at all. §e[*]§r At 8 the deck is at 1.5 km and receives four times the blue light the ground does when the sun is on the horizon, which is what makes clouds still burn while the land has gone cold. §e[*]§r Changes nothing about cloud shape or position.
option.VC_SUN_TINT=Atmospheric Sun Tint
option.VC_SUN_TINT.comment=How strongly the sun reaching each point in a cloud is coloured by the air it travelled through. §e[*]§r Measured per sample and per altitude, so the base of a cloud reddens before its top does. §e[*]§r At 0 every part of every cloud gets the same white sun.
option.VC_AMBIENT_TINT=Sky Colour on Clouds
option.VC_AMBIENT_TINT.comment=How much of the sky's own colour the soft light on a cloud takes. §e[*]§r A cloud's top is lit by the zenith and its base by the ring of sky at the horizon, so at sunset the undersides go orange while the tops stay cool. §e[*]§r Only the colour is taken, never the brightness, so this cannot make clouds lighter or darker. §a0%§r gives every part of the cloud the same flat ambient colour.
option.VC_AERIAL=Distance Haze
option.VC_AERIAL.comment=The air between you and a cloud, taken from the same atmosphere that colours the sun. §e[*]§r Distant clouds keep their shape but lose contrast, and lose blue before red, so far clouds look warmer and hazier - redder still at sunset. §e[*]§r §a100%§r is the physical amount; higher thickens the haze, lower thins it. It changes colour only, not how much of the sky a cloud covers.
option.VC_SUN_STRENGTH=阳光
option.VC_SUN_STRENGTH.comment=Brightness of the directly lit term.
option.VC_AMBIENT_STRENGTH=Ambient
option.VC_AMBIENT_STRENGTH.comment=Brightness of the sky-lit term, which is what keeps the shadowed side from going black. §e[*]§r A stand-in until the sky model arrives.
option.VC_AMBIENT_BOTTOM=Ambient Underside
option.VC_AMBIENT_BOTTOM.comment=How much of the ambient reaches the base of the cloud, as a percentage of the top. §e[*]§r The sky is above, so the underside of a cloud genuinely receives less of it.
option.VC_PHASE_STRENGTH=月相强度
option.VC_PHASE_STRENGTH.comment=Blends between scattering evenly in all directions and the full forward-scattering lobe. §e[*]§r Both ends conserve energy exactly, so this changes where the light goes, never how much there is. §e[*]§r Leave it at 100: with Multiple Scattering on, the higher orders already scatter more evenly and flatten it for you. Turn it down only if you have Multiple Scattering off, where the sun-facing side really is eighty times brighter than the side view.
option.VC_PHASE_G=Forward Lobe
option.VC_PHASE_G.comment=Eccentricity of the forward scattering lobe. §e[*]§r Higher values tighten the silver lining into a smaller ring around the sun.
option.VC_PHASE_BACK=Backward Lobe
option.VC_PHASE_BACK.comment=Eccentricity of the second, backward-facing lobe. §e[*]§r This is the faint glow you see on clouds with the sun behind you.
option.VC_PHASE_MIX=Lobe Balance
option.VC_PHASE_MIX.comment=How much of the phase function is the forward lobe rather than the backward one.
option.VC_LIGHT_SAMPLES=阴影采样数
option.VC_LIGHT_SAMPLES.comment=Samples taken toward the sun at each lit point, to work out how much cloud is in the way. §c[-]§r This is the most expensive setting here, since it multiplies the work at every sample that hits cloud.
option.VC_LIGHT_DETAIL=Light Detail Samples
option.VC_LIGHT_DETAIL.comment=How many of the sun samples read the full cloud field rather than the cheap low-frequency one. §e[*]§r The cheap field has not had its edges eroded away, so it blocks the sun with a cloud denser than the one you are looking at, and cloud edges come out too dark. §e[*]§r Two fixes most of that; going higher costs fetches for little.
option.VC_CONE_SPREAD=Cone Spread
option.VC_CONE_SPREAD.comment=How far the sun samples fan out from the straight line to the sun. §e[*]§r A little of this hides the banding that six samples would otherwise show; too much smears the shadow instead. Measured best around 15.
option.VC_CONE_LENGTH=Cone Length
option.VC_CONE_LENGTH.comment=How far toward the sun those samples reach, as a percentage of the distance at which cloud cores come out neither too bright nor too dark. §e[*]§r It already lengthens on its own as the sun drops and the path through the layer grows. §c[-]§r Shortening it washes out the shadow inside big clouds first, which is the part you notice.
option.VC_SHADOW_STRENGTH=Self-Shadowing
option.VC_SHADOW_STRENGTH.comment=Depth of the shadow a cloud casts on itself. §e[*]§r At 0 clouds are lit as if nothing were in the way.
screen.CLOUD_REALISTIC_MARCH=Quality & Performance
screen.CLOUD_REALISTIC_MARCH.comment=How the clouds are traced. §e[*]§r These trade quality against frame time and change nothing about the clouds themselves.
option.VC_PLANET_RADIUS=Horizon Curvature
option.VC_PLANET_RADIUS.comment=How far away the cloud layer curves down below eye level, set as a planet radius in blocks. §e[*]§r Smaller brings the cloud horizon closer, which ends the layer naturally instead of at a hard edge. §e[*]§r A true Earth radius would be around 800,000 here, which puts the horizon far outside render distance and does nothing at all.
option.VC_REACH=云层距离
option.VC_REACH.comment=How far the march follows a ray before giving up, in blocks. §e[*]§r With Distant Horizons or Voxy the clouds reach at least as far as the LOD terrain, so a longer LOD distance extends them to its edge. §c[-]§r Raising it costs steps on exactly the rays that already cost the most, the ones near the horizon.
option.VC_STEP_GROW=Step Growth
option.VC_STEP_GROW.comment=How much the sample spacing widens with distance. §e[*]§r Close by a cloud edge covers many pixels and needs fine steps; a kilometre out the same edge is one pixel and does not. At 0 the whole budget is spent on detail nobody can resolve.
option.VC_CHEAP_MULT=Empty Space Stride
option.VC_CHEAP_MULT.comment=How much larger the steps are while the ray is in clear air. §e[*]§r In empty space the march samples only the coarse shape and strides through it, then backs up and switches to fine steps the moment it finds something. This is safe because the detail noise only ever removes density, so the coarse value is an upper bound and a coarse miss cannot hide a cloud.
option.VC_EMPTY_EXIT=Exit Patience
option.VC_EMPTY_EXIT.comment=How many empty fine samples in a row before the march goes back to striding. §e[*]§r Too low and it flips modes across every gap inside a cloud; too high and it wastes fine steps in clear air.
screen.CLOUD_REALISTIC_SHAPE=云层形状
screen.CLOUD_REALISTIC_SHAPE.comment=How an individual cloud is built, once the weather map has decided one belongs there.
option.VC_MAP_SPAN=Map Reach
option.VC_MAP_SPAN.comment=How far across the weather map reaches, in blocks. §e[*]§r The map is centred on you and moves with you, so this is a view distance for weather rather than a tile size — it has nothing to do with how big a cloud is, which is Map Size. §c[-]§r Below Cloud Distance the sky ends in a visible circle.
option.VC_MAP_DISTORT=Map Focus
option.VC_MAP_DISTORT.comment=How much of the map’s resolution is pulled in towards you. §e[*]§r 0 spreads it evenly, which wastes most of it on clouds that are a pixel wide at the horizon. At the default the sky overhead is five times sharper than the rim, for the same cost — one map texel is a visible facet along a cloud’s edge, so this is where edge quality comes from.
option.VC_PH_SHAPE_NOISE=Cloud Pattern
option.VC_PH_SHAPE_NOISE.comment=What the sky is cut out of. §e[*]§r §aCellular§r: rounded cells walled off by creases, at three scales - the pattern the reference uses. Clouds come out as many separate heaps with clear sky between them, small ones included. §e[*]§r §aSmooth§r: the previous rolling noise. Its peaks come in clumps, so the same amount of cloud gathers into a few big masses and almost no small clouds form.
value.VC_PH_SHAPE_NOISE.0=平滑
value.VC_PH_SHAPE_NOISE.1=Cellular
option.VC_AC_SIZE=Altocumulus Element Size
option.VC_AC_SIZE.comment=How big one altocumulus element is. §e[*]§r Lower makes a finer mackerel sky with more elements in it, higher makes fewer and larger patches. §e[*]§r It scales both noise frequencies this genus uses, so the pattern keeps its proportions and only changes scale. §e[*]§r The WMO defines altocumulus as elements 1 to 3 degrees wide seen high in the sky, which at this layer's altitude is 17 to 52 blocks across - the default lands at 51. §e[*]§r Going below 35 puts the finest detail under the weather map's texel size near the horizon, where it will soften or shimmer rather than resolve. §e[*]§r Only used by §aCloud Genus: Altocumulus§r.
option.VC_AC_PATCH=Altocumulus Sky Regions
option.VC_AC_PATCH.comment=How much of the sky altocumulus occupies at all. §e[*]§r Real altocumulus arrives in patches - two or three regions a few thousand blocks across, with clear sky between them - and this is the threshold that decides where those regions are. §e[*]§r It reads the pack's own pressure field, so the regions drift, change shape, merge and disappear on their own as the weather moves. §aSynoptic Strength§r and §aWeather Seed§r move them too. §e[*]§r At §a100§r there are no regions and the genus covers the whole sky, which is what it did before this setting existed. §e[*]§r This is separate from §aCloud Coverage§r on purpose: this one says WHERE there is cloud, that one says how full it is once there is. §e[*]§r Only used by §aCloud Genus: Altocumulus§r.
option.VC_CC_SIZE=Cirrocumulus Grain Size
option.VC_CC_SIZE.comment=How big one cirrocumulus grain is. §e[*]§r Lower is finer. All four of this genus's noise taps scale together, so the pattern keeps its proportions. §e[*]§r The WMO defines cirrocumulus as elements under 1 degree wide, which at this altitude is under 40 blocks across. §e[*]§r Only used by §aCloud Genus: Cirrocumulus§r.
option.VC_CC_DETAIL=Cirrocumulus Separation
option.VC_CC_DETAIL.comment=How far apart the grains are cut. §e[*]§r Two rounds of subtraction carve the gaps between individual grains; higher makes them smaller and more separated, lower lets them merge into a sheet. §e[*]§r At 0 the ripple becomes a continuous veil. §e[*]§r Only used by §aCloud Genus: Cirrocumulus§r.
option.VC_CI_SIZE=Cirrus Streak Size
option.VC_CI_SIZE.comment=How large the cirrus streaks are. §e[*]§r Lower gives finer, more numerous fibres; higher gives fewer and broader bands. All of this genus's taps scale together. §e[*]§r Only used by §aCloud Genus: Cirrus§r.
option.VC_CI_FIBRE=Cirrus Fibrousness
option.VC_CI_FIBRE.comment=How far the detail is dragged sideways as it is carved. §e[*]§r This is what makes cirrus fibrous rather than lumpy: each finer round of detail is displaced twice as far as the last, up to most of its own wavelength, so it shears into strands instead of roughening an edge. §e[*]§r At 0 the genus keeps its shape but loses the streaking and reads as patchy haze. It scales with §aCurl Amount§r. §e[*]§r Only used by §aCloud Genus: Cirrus§r.
option.VC_CB_SPACING=Storm Spacing
option.VC_CB_SPACING.comment=How far apart thunderstorms stand, in blocks. §e[*]§r One storm is placed per cell of this size, so at the default of 9000 against a 6000-block render distance there is normally exactly one in view. §e[*]§r Lower crowds them together; higher leaves long stretches of empty sky. §e[*]§r Only used by §aCloud Genus: Cumulonimbus§r.
option.VC_CB_RADIUS=Storm Radius
option.VC_CB_RADIUS.comment=How wide one thunderstorm is, in blocks. §e[*]§r A real supercell is about as wide as it is tall, and this layer is 2272 blocks deep. §e[*]§r The anvil always reaches further than this - it is the tower that this measures. §e[*]§r Only used by §aCloud Genus: Cumulonimbus§r.
option.VC_CB_ANVIL=Anvil Spread
option.VC_CB_ANVIL.comment=How far the top of a thunderstorm flattens out over its own base. §e[*]§r At 0 the storm is a plain domed tower - a §acalvus§r, still growing. §e[*]§r Higher widens the silhouette itself above seven tenths of the height, up to 2.6 times the tower radius, which is the classic §aincus§r overhang. §e[*]§r Below that height nothing changes. §e[*]§r Only used by §aCloud Genus: Cumulonimbus§r.
option.VC_CB_TILT=Storm Lean
option.VC_CB_TILT.comment=How far downwind the top of a thunderstorm leans, as a percentage of its height. §e[*]§r The lean grows with the SQUARE of the height, not in a straight line, because wind shear itself increases with altitude - that is what gives a real storm its bowed profile instead of a leaning-tower one. §e[*]§r At 0 the tower stands straight up. §e[*]§r Only used by §aCloud Genus: Cumulonimbus§r.
option.VC_CB_SWIRL=Mesocyclone Spin
option.VC_CB_SWIRL.comment=How far the storm's texture twists between its base and its top, in hundredths of a radian. §e[*]§r This turns the TURRETS only, never the outline - rotating the outline makes the storm wobble instead of making it rotate. §e[*]§r At 0 the turrets stack straight up the tower. §e[*]§r Only used by §aCloud Genus: Cumulonimbus§r.
option.VC_CB_TURRET=Turret Depth
option.VC_CB_TURRET.comment=How deeply the cauliflower bubbles bite into the side of the tower. §e[*]§r They only eat into the soft outer shoulder; the core of the storm is spared whatever this is set to, or the whole thing perforates into candyfloss. §e[*]§r At 0 the storm is a smooth analytic shape with no surface at all. §e[*]§r Only used by §aCloud Genus: Cumulonimbus§r.
option.VC_CB_CELL=Turret Size
option.VC_CB_CELL.comment=How big one cauliflower bubble is at the body of the storm, in blocks. §e[*]§r The bubbles SHRINK with height on their own, to six tenths of this at the dome: the upper tower is visibly finer-grained than the mid-body in every photograph of a real one. §e[*]§r Only used by §aCloud Genus: Cumulonimbus§r.
option.VC_CB_FLANKS=Flanking Towers
option.VC_CB_FLANKS.comment=How many smaller towers grow out of the side of the storm. §e[*]§r They are ADDED to the main tower, never carved out of it - its outline is left exactly as it is and these only reach further. §e[*]§r Each sits at its own height, straddling the edge of the body so it stays welded on, and the same cauliflower noise runs over both so they read as one cloud. §e[*]§r They crowd the UPWIND side, the way a real supercell's flanking line does, rather than ringing the tower. §e[*]§r At 0 the storm is the bare tower. §e[*]§r Only used by §aCloud Genus: Cumulonimbus§r.
option.VC_CB_FLANK_SIZE=Flanking Tower Size
option.VC_CB_FLANK_SIZE.comment=How big each flanking tower is, as a percentage of the main storm's radius. §e[*]§r Small values read as lumps on the shoulder; large ones as a second tower sharing the same base. §e[*]§r Past about 45 they start to rival the main updraft and the storm stops having one obvious core. §e[*]§r Only used by §aCloud Genus: Cumulonimbus§r.
option.VC_CB_VARIANCE=Density Variance
option.VC_CB_VARIANCE.comment=How much the density varies from place to place INSIDE the storm. §e[*]§r At 0 the interior is one uniform mass and the storm reads as a single lump of cotton wool, because the light march finds the same density wherever it looks. §e[*]§r Higher breaks it into soft patches about 310 blocks across, so the sun reaches different depths in different places and the body gets internal shape. §e[*]§r This is on top of the thinner anvil and the denser updraft core, which are always there. §e[*]§r Only used by §aCloud Genus: Cumulonimbus§r.
option.VC_PH_CELL_FREQ=Cell Density
option.VC_PH_CELL_FREQ.comment=How many cells the Cellular pattern packs into the sky. §e[*]§r Higher gives more, smaller clouds; lower gives fewer, bigger ones. §e[*]§r Only used by §aCloud Pattern: Cellular§r.
option.VC_SHAPE_MODEL=Shape Model
option.VC_SHAPE_MODEL.comment=Which model draws the cloud itself. §e[*]§r §bNubis§r builds density from a cloud-type channel and a measured vertical profile. Its cloud top is one height per cloud, so clouds read as slabs — measured, the type channel alone accounts for 79% of it. §e[*]§r §bPhoton§r ties a column's height to its own coverage instead, so a cloud is tall in the middle and tapers to its edges. §e[*]§r The shape settings below are split: the first group belongs to Photon, the second to Nubis.
value.VC_SHAPE_MODEL.0=Nubis
value.VC_SHAPE_MODEL.1=Photon
option.VC_PH_COV_LOW=Coverage Floor
option.VC_PH_COV_LOW.comment=How much sky is covered where the weather is at its driest. §e[*]§r With the ceiling below it sets the range coverage moves through as the pressure field drifts overhead.
option.VC_PH_COV_HIGH=Coverage Ceiling
option.VC_PH_COV_HIGH.comment=How much sky is covered where the weather is at its wettest. §c[-]§r Past about 70 the gaps close and the deck goes overcast, which also flattens the tops — a column that is fully covered has nothing to taper towards.
option.VC_PH_EGG=Top Carve
option.VC_PH_EGG.comment=How hard density is cut away towards the top of the layer. §e[*]§r This is what makes the cloud an egg rather than a block: it grows with height, so a column runs out at a height set by its own coverage. §e[*]§r Lower gives taller, flatter-topped clouds; higher gives smaller, rounder ones.
option.VC_PH_TOP=Cloud Top Softness
option.VC_PH_TOP.comment=Rounds off the tops of clouds that reach the ceiling of the layer. §e[*]§r The vertical profile does not run out of density on its own at high coverage, so a tall cloud used to be cut by a dead flat horizontal plane where the layer ends. This fades it out over the top slice instead. §e[*]§r It hides the ceiling rather than removing it - for a cloud that should genuinely be taller, raise §aThickness§r. §e[*]§r §a0%§r restores the hard cut.
option.VC_PH_ALT_SCALE=Layer Fill
option.VC_PH_ALT_SCALE.comment=How much of the layer a fully covered column is allowed to fill, as a percentage. §e[*]§r Below 100 the tallest clouds stop short of the ceiling, which is what leaves room for them to differ in height at all.
option.VC_PH_DETAIL_1=Billow Strength
option.VC_PH_DETAIL_1.comment=How deeply the coarse noise bites into the cloud. §e[*]§r It only cuts where the cloud is already thin, never through the core, so it carves lobes into the silhouette rather than punching holes.
option.VC_PH_DETAIL_2=Wisp Strength
option.VC_PH_DETAIL_2.comment=The same, for the finer noise, and it is faded out towards the top of the cloud. §e[*]§r This is the ragged edge along the base and the flanks.
option.VC_PH_TORN=Wind Tearing
option.VC_PH_TORN.comment=Pushes BOTH erosion strengths above their base pair, up to half again. §e[*]§r This is the wind-torn cloud: the reference raises both weights when its weather reports high wind, and that is what tears a cloud into a ragged shape instead of a round heap. §e[*]§r It removes cloud, so raising it thins the sky - put §aCoverage Floor§r back up with it - the floor, not the ceiling: the floor is what lets small clouds form in empty sky, the ceiling grows the big ones into walls if the sky empties out.
option.VC_PH_SHARP_LOW=Base Softness
option.VC_PH_SHARP_LOW.comment=How much the density near the cloud base is lifted towards solid. §e[*]§r Low values leave the base wispy and see-through, which is what a real cumulus does.
option.VC_PH_SHARP_HIGH=Top Solidity
option.VC_PH_SHARP_HIGH.comment=The same at the top of the cloud, where it should be dense and opaque. §e[*]§r The gap between this and Base Softness is what makes a cloud read as solid on top and torn underneath.
option.VC_SHAPE_SCALE=云层大小
option.VC_SHAPE_SCALE.comment=How many blocks across the base cloud shape repeats. Larger means bigger, slower-reading clouds.
option.VC_DETAIL_SCALE=Detail Size
option.VC_DETAIL_SCALE.comment=Scale of the fine noise that eats into the edges. Small values give a fluffier, more broken outline.
option.VC_EROSION_MODE=Erosion Mode
option.VC_EROSION_MODE.comment=Where in the chain the detail noise carves into the cloud, and whether it does at all. §e[*]§r §bAfter Coverage§r is Meteoros' position: the detail bites into a field the coverage step has already thinned, so it wants a very small strength. §e[*]§r §bBefore Coverage§r is the Nubis 2017 position: the detail bites into the raw, dense base noise, which is why the source slide carries no scale factor on it and wants a large strength. §e[*]§r Each is consistent with its own position - a strength tuned for one will shred the cloud in the other. §c[-]§r Before Coverage samples the detail volume for points the coverage step then discards, so it costs a little more.
value.VC_EROSION_MODE.0=§cOFF
value.VC_EROSION_MODE.1=After Coverage
value.VC_EROSION_MODE.2=Before Coverage
option.VC_EROSION=Edge Erosion
option.VC_EROSION.comment=How deeply the detail noise cuts into the cloud. §e[*]§r It moves the upper bound rather than subtracting, which is what keeps the middle of a cloud solid while its edges break up. Measured, subtracting instead drops the average core density from 0.74 to 0.67 and stops any cloud reaching full density at all.
option.VC_ISLANDS=Island Shapes
option.VC_ISLANDS.comment=Blends between the two coverage channels of the weather map. §e[*]§r The second channel is built to add connective tissue between formations, and it can only ever add coverage - so raising this fills in the gaps the gate opens rather than carving islands out. Measured, 0 gives the most separated sky and 100 the most connected one.
option.VC_CURL_AMOUNT=Base Turbulence
option.VC_CURL_AMOUNT.comment=How much swirling flow distorts the detail near the cloud base, as a fraction of one detail tile at the strongest point of the flow. §e[*]§r 50 puts the displacement in the same band as the source model, 0 to about half a tile, with a mean near a quarter. Past 100 it stops roughening the surface and starts shearing the erosion into long curtains. §e[*]§r It fades out with height, because it is turbulence at the condensation level rather than a property of the whole cloud.
option.VC_CURL_SCALE=Turbulence Size
option.VC_CURL_SCALE.comment=Scale of the swirls that distort the cloud base.
option.VC_SKEW=Wind Skew
option.VC_SKEW.comment=How far the top of a cloud leans downwind from its base, as a percentage of the layer thickness. §e[*]§r Real clouds lean, because the wind is faster higher up. 0 gives vertical columns. §c[-]§r Past roughly half the thickness the top and the base start sampling unrelated noise and the cloud stops being one object -- it smears into streaks with no vertical structure at all.
option.VC_ANVIL=Anvil Spread
option.VC_ANVIL.comment=How far a cloud flares out as it approaches the top of the layer, the way a cumulus spreads into an anvil. §e[*]§r It is the only control here that changes a cloud's width with height; everything else acts on the whole column at once. §e[*]§r Below 70% of the layer height it does nothing at all, and it reaches full strength at 80%. §e[*]§r At 0 the cloud has the same silhouette from base to top.
option.VC_DEBUG_CHANNEL=Debug Channel
option.VC_DEBUG_CHANNEL.comment=Which channel the debug view shows. §e[*]§r The index means one thing for the noise slice and another for the weather map, so each name lists both. §e[*]§r On the weather map anything above 3 shows all channels at once as colour. §e[*]§r The shape texture is 128 cubed: red is Perlin-Worley, and green, blue and alpha are Worley at rising frequency. The detail texture is 32 cubed Worley. §e[*]§r Getting these in the wrong order is the classic way to build a cloud that looks almost right and never can be, so they are all viewable separately.
value.VC_DEBUG_CHANNEL.0=0 Perlin-Worley / Coverage
value.VC_DEBUG_CHANNEL.1=1 Worley Low / Islands
value.VC_DEBUG_CHANNEL.2=2 Worley Mid / Type
value.VC_DEBUG_CHANNEL.3=3 Worley High / Bottom Type
value.VC_DEBUG_CHANNEL.4=4 Detail R / All
value.VC_DEBUG_CHANNEL.5=5 Detail G / Pressure
value.VC_DEBUG_CHANNEL.6=6 Detail B / Wind
value.VC_DEBUG_CHANNEL.7=7 Curl / All
option.VC_DEBUG_TILES=Debug Tiling
option.VC_DEBUG_TILES.comment=How many times the slice is repeated across the screen in Debug View 1. §e[*]§r At 2 or more, any seam in the noise shows up as a straight line where the copies meet. There should not be one: the noise wraps its own lattice, so it is periodic by construction rather than by luck.
option.VC_DEBUG_SLICE_SPEED=Debug Slice Speed
option.VC_DEBUG_SLICE_SPEED.comment=How fast Debug View 1 travels through the depth of the texture. §e[*]§r At 0 the slice is frozen. The slice must change as it travels -- a still image means the third dimension never got written.
option.VC_THICKNESS=Layer Thickness
option.VC_THICKNESS.comment=How many blocks tall the cloud layer is. §e[*]§r Cloud Altitude is the BASE of the layer for this style, not its middle, so the deck grows upward from it.
option.VC_COVERAGE=覆盖范围
option.VC_COVERAGE.comment=How much of the sky the layer fills. §e[*]§r Until the shape stages are built this is a flat overcast deck, so raising it only thickens the same sheet.
option.VC_DENSITY=密度
option.VC_DENSITY.comment=How strongly the layer absorbs light along the view ray.
option.VC_STEP_SIZE=步长
option.VC_STEP_SIZE.comment=Distance in blocks between samples along the ray. §c[-]§r Smaller is more accurate and much slower. This is the setting that decides how clean cloud edges are; raising it to 8 makes a fifth of all edge pixels visibly wrong. §e[*]§r The default 8 is chosen for §aCloud TAA§r on: each frame samples the cloud at a random offset and many frames average out, which measures twice as accurate as a step of 4 in a single frame, at half the cost. With Cloud TAA off, set this back to §4§r.
option.VC_MAX_STEPS=Step Limit
option.VC_MAX_STEPS.comment=Hard cap on samples per ray, so a grazing view along the layer cannot cost an unbounded amount. §e[*]§r Only reached when flying inside the cloud layer; lowering it starts cutting clouds short there first. Use Debug View 5 to see where.
option.VC_TEMPORAL=Cloud TAA
option.VC_TEMPORAL.comment=Accumulates the clouds over many frames instead of trusting one. §e[*]§r Smoother, calmer clouds with less grain and shimmer, especially far away. §e[*]§r Works on top of the pack's own TAA, so it needs TAA switched on in Anti-Aliasing. §e[*]§r Off restores exactly the old behaviour. Not active with Distant Horizons. §e[*]§r It also pays for itself: the march runs at half the samples because of it (§aStep Size§r 8), so turning it off costs frame rate as well as smoothness.
value.VC_TEMPORAL.0=关
value.VC_TEMPORAL.1=开
option.VC_TEMPORAL_FRAMES=Cloud TAA Frames
option.VC_TEMPORAL_FRAMES.comment=How many frames of history the clouds are averaged over while the camera is still. §e[*]§r More is smoother; fewer reacts faster when clouds change. The history drops out automatically when the view moves, so fast turns do not smear. §e[*]§r Only used with §aCloud TAA§r on.
option.CLOUD_ALT1=云层高度
option.CLOUD_ALT1.comment=Determines the world height which the clouds will appear at. §e[*]§r For "Realistic" this is the bottom of the volumetric layer, and the layer reaches Cloud Thickness blocks above it. §e[*]§r This setting does nothing with the "Vanilla" Cloud Style.

option.CLOUD_SPEED_MULT=云层速度
option.CLOUD_SPEED_MULT.comment=决定云层移动速度。"同步世界时间"设置使用《我的世界》世界时间推进云层位置，使同服务器玩家看到相同云景。[*] 此设置仅适用于"重制"或"无约束"云层样式。
value.CLOUD_SPEED_MULT.100=同步至世界时间
value.TIME_TRANSITION_SPEED.0.0=关闭（即时生效）

option.CLOUD_R=云层红色倍增器
option.CLOUD_G=云层绿色倍增器
option.CLOUD_B=云层蓝色倍增器

option.CLOUD_UNBOUND_AMOUNT=云量
option.CLOUD_UNBOUND_AMOUNT.comment=调整无边界云的数量。

option.CLOUD_UNBOUND_SIZE_MULT=云朵精细度
option.CLOUD_UNBOUND_SIZE_MULT.comment=调整不受约束云层的大小。数值越高，云层越小。

option.CLOUD_UNBOUND_RAIN_ADD=雨云增益
option.CLOUD_UNBOUND_RAIN_ADD.comment=调整雨雪天气下应用的云量增加幅度。
prefix.CLOUD_UNBOUND_RAIN_ADD=+
option.CLOUD_RAIN_OVERCAST=雨天阴云
option.CLOUD_RAIN_OVERCAST.comment=控制雨水完全填补无界云层间隙的程度。[*] 上方的雨云增厚选项使云层更厚重；此选项则让它们相互融合。[*] 0 = 旧版零散的风暴天空，可见蓝色天空。[*] 80 = 近乎密实的阴云层。[*] 仅在雨雪天气时生效。
suffix.UBP2_FLATNESS=%
suffix.UBP2_AMOUNT=%
suffix.UBP2_SIZE=%
suffix.UBP2_OPACITY=%
suffix.UBP2_SPEED=%
suffix.UBP2_RAIN_ADD=%
suffix.UBP2_RAIN_OVERCAST=%
suffix.PP_FILTER_I=%
suffix.CLOUD_RAIN_OVERCAST=%

option.CLOUD_LAYER2=第二云层
option.CLOUD_LAYER2.comment=Draw a second deck of clouds at its own altitude. §e[*]§r Works with Reimagined and Unbound. Unbound+ has its own upper deck already and is left to it, and Realistic builds its layers from the cloud type instead. §e[*]§r The two decks are blended properly rather than one hiding the other, so thin gaps in the near deck show the far one through them, and either can be the one in front depending on where you are standing. §c[-]§r Costs a second cloud march. OFF by default.
value.CLOUD_LAYER2.0=关
value.CLOUD_LAYER2.1=开

option.CLOUD_ALT2=云层高度2
option.CLOUD_ALT2.comment=World height of the second cloud deck. §e[*]§r Only does anything with Second Cloud Layer on. It can sit above or below the first -- which deck is drawn in front follows from where you are and which way you are looking.

option.NETHER_STORM=下界风暴
option.NETHER_STORM.comment=为下界维度添加类似烟雾的体积效果。[-] 此效果可能影响性能。

option.NETHER_STORM_I=下界风暴不透明度
option.NETHER_STORM_I.comment=调整下界风暴的不透明度。

option.NETHER_STORM_LOWER_ALT=下界风暴起始高度
option.NETHER_STORM_LOWER_ALT.comment=决定下界风暴的起始下限高度。低于此高度不会出现风暴，紧邻此高度上方的区域看起来最明亮。

option.NETHER_STORM_HEIGHT=下界风暴高度
option.NETHER_STORM_HEIGHT.comment=决定下界风暴的形成高度。降低此值将导致风暴聚集在“下界风暴起始高度”附近。

option.NETHER_VIEW_LIMIT=下界视野限制
option.NETHER_VIEW_LIMIT.comment=决定下界迷雾允许的最大可视距离。[-] 启用"下界风暴"时，增大此设置可能影响性能。
value.NETHER_VIEW_LIMIT.96.0=6区块
