option.TONEMAPPER=色调映射
option.TONEMAPPER.comment=The curve that fits the scene's light onto your screen - it decides contrast, how highlights roll off to white and how colours behave on the way. §e[*]§r Every tonemapper is pre-exposed so mid-grey lands exactly where the Original puts it: switching changes the character of the image, not its brightness. §e[*]§r §aOriginal§r is the pack's own look. §bAgX§r and §bACES§r are film-like, §bPBR Neutral§r keeps colours truest, §bGran Turismo§r and §bLottes§r are the most adjustable, §bReinhard§r is the classic soft one. §e[*]§r Settings of each are in Tonemapper Settings.
value.TONEMAPPER.0=§aOriginal
value.TONEMAPPER.1=None (Clip)
value.TONEMAPPER.2=reinhard
value.TONEMAPPER.3= reinhard extended
value.TONEMAPPER.4=Reinhard Luminance
value.TONEMAPPER.5=莱因哈德-乔迪映射
value.TONEMAPPER.6=指数衰减
value.TONEMAPPER.7=Hejl-Burgess-Dawson
value.TONEMAPPER.8=Hable（神秘海域2）
value.TONEMAPPER.9=Hejl 2015 色调映射
value.TONEMAPPER.10=ACES (Fast)
value.TONEMAPPER.11=ACES (Fitted)
value.TONEMAPPER.12=洛特斯
value.TONEMAPPER.13=Gran Turismo
value.TONEMAPPER.14=AGX
value.TONEMAPPER.15=PBR 中性
option.TONE_WHITE=白点
option.TONE_WHITE.comment=How bright the scene has to get before it reaches pure white, as a share of the curve's own default. §e[*]§r Used by Reinhard Extended, Reinhard Luminance, Hable and Hejl 2015. Higher keeps more detail in bright skies and lights; lower turns them white sooner.
suffix.TONE_WHITE=%
option.TONE_SPACE=Tonemap Color Space
option.TONE_SPACE.comment=The colour space the tonemapper's curve runs in. The picture goes in, the curve runs, the picture comes straight back out - nothing else changes. §e[*]§r A per-channel curve bends bright colours towards pure red, green or blue as it compresses them: fire goes yellow, sky goes cyan. The wider the space, the truer the hue stays: a very bright light fades towards white instead of towards yellow or cyan. §e[*]§r §bRec.709§r is the screen's own. §bDisplay P3§r is a little wider, §bRec.2020§r and §bACEScg§r wider still, §bACES 2065-1§r and §bCIE XYZ§r the widest. §e[*]§r No effect on ACES (Fitted), AgX, Reinhard Luminance, PBR Neutral, Hejl-Burgess-Dawson and None, which handle colour their own way.
value.TONE_SPACE.0=Rec.709 (screen)
value.TONE_SPACE.1=ACEScg
value.TONE_SPACE.2=ACES 2065-1
value.TONE_SPACE.3=Display P3
value.TONE_SPACE.4=Rec.2020
value.TONE_SPACE.5=CIE XYZ
option.CC_OUTPUT_SPACE=Monitor Color Space
option.CC_OUTPUT_SPACE.comment=The colour space of your monitor. §e[*]§r Leave it on §bsRGB§r unless you know otherwise: that is right for almost every monitor, and for any monitor your system already colour-manages. §e[*]§r A wide-gamut monitor (many OLED and QD-OLED panels) in its native mode shows an sRGB picture oversaturated - grass goes neon, reds glow. Choose the monitor's own space, usually §bDisplay P3§r, and the colours come back to what they should be. §e[*]§r If the picture turns washed out, the monitor was already in its sRGB mode: set this back. §e[*]§r Applied last, after every effect. No measurable cost.
value.CC_OUTPUT_SPACE.0=sRGB
value.CC_OUTPUT_SPACE.1=Display P3
value.CC_OUTPUT_SPACE.2=Adobe RGB
value.CC_OUTPUT_SPACE.3=Rec.2020
